('0f17c9',4,'Combinatorics',['Residue classes','Extremal bounds'],'Pair residue classes differing by k; a pair of class sizes x,y contributes xy(x+y)≤(x+y)³/4. Concentrating all entries in one balanced pair attains 2n³.'),
('c765b1',5,'Combinatorics',['Subset sums','Greedy algorithm','Information'],'The smallest 2^(n−2) subset sums reveal the first n−1 entries by greedy multiset cancellation, since n−2 entries generate too few nonempty sums; the full sum then reveals the last.'),
('c63ac9',6,'Combinatorics',['Graph theory','Extremal arguments','Construction'],'A maximum edge constrains every other vertex to a degenerate triangle; duplicated distances force an exceptional four-vertex configuration, which a fifth vertex rules out. This yields n−1 labels, except two suffice for n=4.'),
('a41d6d',4.5,'Combinatorics',['Permutations','Induction','Construction'],'The column containing n correct entries constrains small n; a four-row base construction extends by appending a fixed new column and one derangement row. Precisely n≥4 work.'),
('db5165',4.5,'Algebra',['Telescoping','Extremal bounds','Equality cases'],'The complements of the ratios have product one half, so their minimum and maximum bracket 2^(−1/n). Equality gives an explicit geometric progression of the x_i.'),
('462ae5',7,'Number Theory',['Multiplicative order','Primes','Squares','Valuations'],'Even exponents are trapped between consecutive squares. For odd n, the largest prime divisor leads through a prime q≡3 modulo 4 and order 2p to a larger prime divisor of n, a substantial number-theoretic contradiction.'),
('7c9b7e',6.5,'Number Theory',['Powers of two','Congruences','Coprimality'],'Parity and pairwise coprimality permit modular inversion at the smallest power of two; residues of squares force all variables too large unless the smallest is one, after which two explicit families remain.'),
('abf430',6,'Combinatorics',['Combinatorial geometry','Colouring','Extremal arguments'],'Separating the points into two sets of five gives a three-colour lower bound on each side via monochromatic triangles; a regular decagon construction reaches six colours.'),
('90f8ad',4.5,'Combinatorics',['Double counting','Set systems','Involutions'],'Positive membership counts must be distinct because undirected adjacency is symmetric. Their sum is bounded by n(n+1)/2, attained by the nested initial segments.'),
('510aa3',6,'Geometry',['Coordinates','Circumcentres','Tangency'],'Orthogonality of the two diameter pairs gives a linear relation between the horizontal coordinates of two auxiliary circumcentres; convexity controls their opposite signs, producing the absolute length identity.'),
